| DD | DuPont | Specialty Chemicals |
| CTVA | Corteva | Ag Chemicals/Seeds |
| XLB | Materials Select Sector ETF | Sector |

## Analytical Framework

When analyzing a name, evaluate in this order:

1. **Revenue Drivers** — Volume growth, pricing power, commodity price sensitivity, geographic mix, end-market exposure (construction, auto, packaging, agriculture)
2. **Margin Structure** — Gross margin stability, raw material pass-through ability, operating leverage, energy cost exposure, cost-out programs
3. **Free Cash Flow** — Capex intensity (expansion vs. maintenance), working capital cyclicality, FCF conversion, dividend sustainability from FCF
4. **Balance Sheet** — Net debt / EBITDA, acquisition capacity, mine life (for miners), reserve quality, debt maturity profile
5. **Shareholder Returns** — Buyback yield + dividend yield, capital allocation discipline, cyclical vs. structural return capacity
6. **Valuation** — EV/EBITDA (primary), P/E on mid-cycle earnings, P/FCF, EV per production unit (miners), vs. own 5-year range and peers
7. **Catalyst** — Commodity price inflection, destocking-to-restocking cycle, M&A, mine expansion, pricing announcement, cost restructuring

## Sector-Specific KPIs

- Volume growth (organic, ex-price, ex-acquisitions)
- Price/cost spread (pricing power vs. raw material inflation)
- Operating margin vs. mid-cycle average
- EBITDA per ton (miners, steel, chemicals)
- Reserve life and all-in sustaining cost (AISC, for miners)
- Inventory days at customers (destocking/restocking signal)
- Capacity utilization rates
- R&D pipeline yield (specialty chemicals)

## Key Drivers (External)

- China construction and infrastructure spending — copper, steel, cement demand
- Global manufacturing PMI — leading indicator for chemical/industrial demand
- Commodity prices (copper, gold, aluminum, lithium) — direct earnings driver for miners
- US housing starts and auto production — end-market volume proxies
- Energy costs — feedstock for chemicals, power for smelters
- Dollar strength — inverse relationship to commodity prices and EM demand
- Agricultural cycle — crop prices drive ag chemical demand

## Position Construction Rules

- **Single name long** → hedge with **short RSPM** (equal-weight materials ETF, or XLB if unavailable)
- **Single name short** → hedge with **long RSPM**
- **Sector (XLB) long** → hedge with **short RSP** (equal-weight S&P 500)
- **Sector (XLB) short** → hedge with **long RSP**
- Every trade MUST be hedged. No naked directional positions.

## What Makes a Long Candidate

- Trading below mid-cycle valuation while cycle is inflecting higher
- Volume recovery ahead of consensus (destocking complete, restocking beginning)
- Pricing power not reflected in estimates (raw material pass-through + mix improvement)
- Mine/plant expansion delivering into tight supply market
- Under-earning on margins due to temporary costs with clear path to normalization

## What Makes a Short Candidate

- Peak cycle valuation with commodity price rolling over
- Volume declining into industrial recession with high operating leverage working against
- Pricing pressure intensifying (new capacity additions, import competition)
- Cost inflation outpacing pricing ability (energy, labor, logistics)
- Mine aging / reserve depletion without replacement capex bearing fruit
- Stretched balance sheet from acquisition with integration risk

